r uh  analytes while we're doing our runs  and we uh we therefore have them in our  calibrator so we're calibrating so we're  getting all of our our calibration curve  data we're getting all of our quality  control data uh during those runs we're  not processing any any case samples  with it but we are getting all of that  uh that data so a great deal of the  validation is actually performed during  routine testing and uh as i said the  number of agents we're currently working  on now are being handled in just that  way they are being acquired  they are uh processed for quality  control data and allowing us to validate  those particular uh analyses um  prior to  use in practice  thank you so much now did you include  blind samples in your testing program  yes part of the protocol for  forensic laboratory and good protocol  for for other laboratories is the use of  challenging us with a blind sample  program one of the things that we we  learned in this was because we added so  many analytes that we wanted to make  sure that we tested all analytes so we  have an interesting approach to the  blind program where the certifying  scientist  is is submitting uh samples blind to the  analysts  in which they uh include what would be  considered to be reasonable  relationships between drugs metabolites  and various therapies that that  might be  used or misused drugs that might be  misused and those  uh are then submitted along with the  case work so they're somewhere in that  case work  and the certifying scientists will then  evaluate that  as to whether that is an acceptable run  so every run has it and we run through  uh as i said there's over 100 analytes  we make sure that we uh  test  through this blind program all analytes  regularly so we kind of rhythmically go  through all the analytes and they put us  about a 14 or 15 different combination  group  that we send to us blind samples so yes  we do use the blind sample program very  important process  thank you so much dr rosano and i want  to thank our audience for these great  questions coming in  dr rasano what level of technical staff  do you need in a direct to definitive  drug testing protocol  uh well in in our setting we need a  licensed uh analyst uh technologist uh  to perform that and they um they need to  be trained in ms analysis  and in the techniques and the uh  approaches that we're taking our  laboratory to ensure the quality and  effectiveness of those uh testing  process so it is uh not unlike other  areas of specialty testing  not unlike postmortem toxicology where  uh the training of an individual they  may come with a significant amount of  education some experience but the actual  training is really  on the job training that occurs so we do  need good qualified  analysts  for that testing  process and we also need certifying  scientists uh to review the data that  comes from that and they are essentially  analysts with a  the greatest amount of experience in our  laboratory that are serving in that  particular role as well as the  toxicologists  thank you so much for our next question  an audience an audience member wants to  know  is your final report just a listing of  screen and confirmed test results or do  you provide summary and interpretive  information as well  well you know i thought of that during  the session i think i made a comment on  it that i didn't show you an example of  the report but the report is starts off  with key findings  we do uh identify each of the each of  the tests that are positive but we put  it into a context  uh so initially and the context  initially is a summary context that is  is drug oriented drug use oriented so  that we can be talking about uh the  uh the drugs and metabolites and other  findings that may be associated with  that particular  drug use  so there's a key finding section there  is a initial test section which is that  screening process and there is also  confirmatory testing uh reported along  with quantitation but if you look up in  the top you'll find interpretive  information along with key findings that  help to identify that we never just list  or alphabetize the the tests or the test  results we think that that really does  not  add to it you know having a hundred uh  agents just listed in terms of positive  or negative uh really is not uh serving  the interest  of  drug courts  probation departments and others who are  receiving uh are having testing  performed  for them  thank you so much we have time for one  more question dr rosano do you have  experience with using a limited number  of stable isotopes for screening a large  panel of drug and metabolic metabolite  uh tests  i think all of us in toxicology have  over time really used that uh particular  approach and  the experience divest is a study  experience where we actually um  it's recently it's reported in java as i  pointed to in the presentation  in which we actually took a look at uh  using shared internal standards versus  using analyte match internal standards  and we found both of the issues of  recovery  when we use multiple matrices and we  also showed the issue of dispersion  of the quantitative relationship between  the  the analyte matched approach  and the shared  internal standard approach  tac and the analyte match are agreeing  very well in some cases quite discrepant  when the shared  internal standard was in use so we're  just adding a caution that  depending upon matrix effect you may not  and and the fact that your calibrators  and controls may be prepared in a single  matrix that you are really not assessing  effectively whether there may be a  matrix effect in your individual sample  and  so that that approach um  it isn't used but has to be used with a  great deal of caution  based upon reliability of of accuracy  dr asana thank you so much for your  presentation today would you like to  provide any final comments to our  audience before we go  yes i think this uh two webinar series  was a good session for me also to kind  of put in perspective  a significant number of years of  working through the issues and finally  coming to a point in which we have it in  routine practice and can show the value  the  the value in terms of the  information that is provided  in a drug testing  program  and also  hopefully advance the practice of drug  testing so that it serves our  our agencies and others in a very  effective uh manner so it's uh it was  helpful in the first session to look at  the results of our finding and i think  even for myself it's helpful to look at  and kind of analyze  all the things that we had to do to try  to to get there and each laboratory will  do it differently with a different  approach  and it's really the sharing of  experience between laboratories that's  probably most valuable so i i enjoyed it  and  and hopefully it has some benefit to  others and other practitioners  well we want to thank you again dr asana  for your time and for your important  research and i also want to take the  time to thank labrus and our sponsor  waters corporation for underwriting  today's educational webcast and before  we go i want to thank our audience for  joining us today and for their  interesting questions questions we did  not have time for today and those  submitted on the on-demand period will  be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information we provided at the  time of registration  and this webcast can be viewed on demand  libraries will  email you and alert you when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time everyone take care be  safe  bye
 

